00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.400
COLT MCANLIS: For a
company like TimeSliders,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.890
time series data is the backbone
of their business model,

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.910
but lately, it's been
the bane of their users.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.550
Their main product is a
performance instrumentation

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.100
library that developers can
add to their mobile apps

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.200
to get runtime data
from the field.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.570
Sadly, this process of
building and displaying

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.430
performance tables in
the report web pages

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.250
quickly became a bottleneck and
a less-than-ideal experience

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:36.840
for the majority
of their clients.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.256
Thankfully, we
didn't have to look

00:00:38.256 --> 00:00:39.690
too far to find the problem--

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:40.560
timestamps.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.450
Now see, every 100
milliseconds or so,

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.400
their library would
log the battery status,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.430
creating an entry
into Bigtable, where

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.120
the row key
consisted of the word

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.310
"battery" plus a timestamp.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.310
Now, while this seems like a
straightforward implementation,

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.650
it runs into a problem
with hot-spotting.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.990
Now see, when a row
key for a time series

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.270
includes a timestamp,
then all of your writes

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:01.540
will target a single node.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.630
It'll fill that node,
and then move on

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.200
to the next node in the cluster,
filling that node, and so on.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.300
This pattern creates
scenarios where performance

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.460
suffers for your application.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.380
Addressing this problem
means leveraging

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.540
the concept of field promotion.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.510
Now, this trick involves moving
fields from the column data

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.742
into the row key to make
writes noncontiguous.

00:01:21.742 --> 00:01:23.880
Now, for example,
TimeSliders ended up

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.030
promoting the user ID
value from a column

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.050
to an element of the row key.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.260
This change solved their
hot-spotting issue because

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.780
of the fact that the
identifier for users

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.517
provided a more uniform
distribution of row keys,

00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:38.850
splitting writes across
multiple nodes in their cluster,

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.700
reducing hot-spotting,
and improving performance.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.844
Now, in case field promotion
doesn't work for you,

00:01:44.844 --> 00:01:47.010
then check out the article
linked in the description

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.350
below, where we cover a
few other alternatives.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.720
Likewise, don't forget to
subscribe to our YouTube

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.910
channel for more great
Cloud Performance Atlas

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.170
content, because when
it comes to performance,

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.660
every millisecond counts.

